+            <article class="writing-piece-article" id="read-story" aria-labelledby="story-title">
+                <header class="writing-story-header">
+                    <h2 id="story-title">Where I’m From</h2>
+                    <p class="writing-story-author">by <a href="/about-me/" rel="author">Aaron L. Schlossberg</a></p>
+                </header>
+
+                <div class="writing-story" data-reading-body data-search-body>
+                    <p>I’m from the cul-de-sac where puddles don’t dry,<br>
+                    from the palm leaf that fell from a sky that doesn’t lie;<br>
+                    I’m from the bow that hums when the heater kicks on,<br>
+                    from the arrows I never loosed, just dreamed had gone;</p>
+
+                    <p>I’m from the drain that breathes under turquoise light,<br>
+                    from daring my reflection to blink first at night;<br>
+                    I’m from Mom’s cracked mug that smells like cloves,<br>
+                    from Dad’s half-built shelf and his half-told jokes;</p>
+
+                    <p>I’m from messages sent at 3:04,<br>
+                    from reading them back like confessions or lore;<br>
+                    I’m from the frog in the gutter that sang in the rain,<br>
+                    from the sidewalk chalk spelling my old nickname;</p>
+
+                    <p>I’m from Tiana framed between thumbtacks and tape,<br>
+                    from Anika’s autograph looping like a secret escape;<br>
+                    I’m from the bus stop where the lamp hums loud,<br>
+                    from counting how many moths make a crowd;</p>
+
+                    <p>I’m from the place where no one says “stay”,<br>
+                    from the voice that fills the silence before it decays;<br>
+                    I’m from the night I mistook fog for a ghost,<br>
+                    from learning that echoes tell truths the loudest voice won’t;</p>
+
+                    <p>I’m from chlorine-stung eyes and the fear of the deep,<br>
+                    from a dream where the pool drain whispered in sleep;<br>
+                    I’m from my neighbor’s cat who stares like she knows,<br>
+                    from the cracked snow globe still making snow;</p>
+
+                    <p>I’m from notebooks of words I meant to say,<br>
+                    from eraser dust that still spells my name halfway;<br>
+                    I’m from ceiling-fan shadows that count my sins,<br>
+                    from tracing my breath on the cold within;</p>
+
+                    <p>I’m from the clock that skips the number four,<br>
+                    from a knock that came once and nevermore;<br>
+                    I’m from the promise to stay when I wanted to run,<br>
+                    from the soft hum of streetlights impersonating the sun;</p>
+
+                    <p>I’m from the rain that plays piano on tin,<br>
+                    from knocking over the art room’s trash bin;<br>
+                    I’m from the attic where nothing’s been sold,<br>
+                    from jars of air labeled <em>Young</em> and <em>Old</em>;</p>
+
+                    <p>I’m from the phone that rings when only Joyce is there,<br>
+                    from answering anyway just to prove I care;<br>
+                    I’m from the photo booth strip where my eyes are closed,<br>
+                    from the second shot where I almost look whole;</p>
+
+                    <p>I’m from the words I rehearse before sleep,<br>
+                    from the small forgivenesses I never keep;<br>
+                    I’m from the streetlight that flickers when I pass,<br>
+                    from believing it’s trying to tell me “<em>at last</em>”;</p>
+
+                    <p>I’m from nostalgic aesthetics that now scream “<em>liminal!</em>”,<br>
+                    from forty-five whole seconds on that goddamn bull;<br>
+                    I’m from constantly yapping ‘til the day is done,<br>
+                    and now from learning that silence is NOT where I’m from.</p>
+
+                    <hr class="writing-scene-break">
+
+                    <p><strong>Author’s note</strong>: “<a href="http://www.georgeellalyon.com/where.html"><em>Where I’m From</em></a>” was originally a poem by George Ella Lyon. I have made my own version before (which can be accessed <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1z0DHCxZqntPS-49qIjbiPsJ6sSGq9V0tu1GwmJvk9fY/edit"><em>here</em></a>); however, for <em>this</em> assignment, it was for my “Intro to Fiction Writing” course, so I didn’t think that I should just reproduce my own. It had to be <em>fictional</em>, whereas mine is truthful and about <em>me</em>. So what I did is imagine a new, fictional person, and write what <em>their</em> “Where I’m From” would be. I <em>did</em> insert a bit of prose that applies to me as well, but it is mostly fictional.</p>
+                </div>
